I'll provide you with a Java code implementation to read the CSV data, sort the planets by their distance from the sun, and print the sorted list to the console.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.List;

class Planet {
    String name;
    double distance;

    public Planet(String name, double distance) {
        this.name = name;
        this.distance = distance;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "Planet{name='" + name + "', distance=" + distance + "}";
    }
}

public class PlanetsSorting {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Planet> planets = parseCSVData();
        planets.sort(Comparator.comparingDouble(planet -> planet.distance));
        printPlanets(planets);
    }

    private static List<Planet> parseCSVData() {
        String csvData = "Mercury,57.91\n" +
                         "Venus,108.2\n" +
                         "Earth,149.6\n" +
                         "Mars,227.9\n" +
                         "Jupiter,778.3\n" +
                         "Saturn,1427\n" +
                         "Uranus,2871\n" +
                         "Neptune,4497";

        List<Planet> planets = new ArrayList<>();

        String[] lines = csvData.split("\n");
        for (String line : lines) {
            String[] values = line.split(",");
            String name = values[0];
            double distance = Double.parseDouble(values[1]);
            planets.add(new Planet(name, distance));
        }
        return planets;
    }

    private static void printPlanets(List<Planet> planets) {
        for (Planet planet : planets) {
            System.out.println(planet);
        }
    }
}
```

When you run this code, you'll get the desired sorted list of planets as follows:

```
